## Description

An already-open consumer changed outside OMP is not synchronized before renaming a symbol in its unchanged declaration file. The language server returns ranges for the old consumer text; OMP applies them to current disk text and reports success. In this reproduction, `answer → respond` writes `respond` into two blank lines, leaves the real import and both calls unchanged, and renames the declaration, breaking both files.

Reproduced through upstream `LspTool.execute` with two real servers: TypeScript native 7.0.2 (`tsc --lsp --stdio`) and basedpyright 1.40.1 (pyright 1.1.414). This is the **non-target open consumer** case, not the target-file reconciliation fixed by #11419.

## Steps to Reproduce

In a prepared upstream checkout at `2ee514247f` (dependencies and native module built), use the existing `node_modules/.bin/tsc` with LSP support, version 7.0.2. Save the following as `repro-rename.ts` at the repository root and run `bun repro-rename.ts`. It creates only a local fixture and calls the production tool directly; no provider or HTTP service is needed. Use a fresh fixture directory if repeating.

```ts
import { mkdir } from "node:fs/promises";
import path from "node:path";
import { LspTool } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/tool.ts";
import { Settings } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/config/settings.ts";
import { shutdownAll } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/client.ts";
import { initTheme } from "./packages/tui/src/theme/theme.ts";
import type { LspParams } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/types.ts";
import type { ToolSession } from "./packages/coding-agent/src/tools/types.ts";

const cwd = path.resolve(".lsp-rename-repro");
await mkdir(cwd, { recursive: true });
await Bun.write(path.join(cwd, "package.json"), '{"private":true,"type":"module"}');
await Bun.write(path.join(cwd, "tsconfig.json"), JSON.stringify({
  compilerOptions: { strict: true, module: "nodenext", moduleResolution: "nodenext" },
  include: ["*.ts"],
}));
await Bun.write(path.join(cwd, "lsp.json"), JSON.stringify({ servers: {
  "typescript-language-server": { disabled: true },
  "typescript-native": {
    command: path.resolve("node_modules/.bin/tsc"), args: ["--lsp", "--stdio"],
    fileTypes: [".ts"], rootMarkers: ["package.json"],
  },
} }));
const a = path.join(cwd, "a.ts"), b = path.join(cwd, "b.ts");
const consumer = 'import { answer } from "./a.js";\nexport const value = answer();\n';
await Bun.write(a, "export function answer(): number { return 42; }\n");
await Bun.write(b, consumer);
await initTheme("dark");
const tool = new LspTool({ cwd, settings: Settings.isolated() } as ToolSession);
async function call(args: LspParams) {
  const result = await tool.execute("repro", { timeout: 60, ...args });
  for (const part of result.content) if (part.type === "text") console.log(part.text);
  console.log(result.details);
}
try {
  await call({ action: "symbols", file: b }); // Open consumer's original text.
  await call({ action: "references", file: a, line: 1, symbol: "answer" });
  // Change disk without going through OMP's write/edit tools.
  await Bun.write(b, "\n\n" + consumer + "export const newValue = answer();\n");
  await call({ action: "references", file: a, line: 1, symbol: "answer" });
  await call({ action: "rename", file: a, line: 1, symbol: "answer", new_name: "respond" });
  console.log("a.ts:\n" + await Bun.file(a).text());
  console.log("b.ts:\n" + await Bun.file(b).text());
} finally {
  await shutdownAll();
}
```

## Expected Behavior

Rename should preserve the two blank lines, rename the declaration, and change the consumer import and both calls to `respond`. References should include the newly added call and use the consumer's current line numbers.

Synchronize relevant open documents before cross-file operations, or refuse/recompute edits when the server snapshot differs from disk. Do not report a successfully applied rename while writing identifiers at obsolete positions.

## Error Output

No exception. After the external edit, references still report `Found 3 reference(s)` at `a.ts:1:17`, `b.ts:1:10`, and `b.ts:2:22`, instead of four references with consumer locations on lines 3–5. Rename returns `success: true` and:

```text
Applied rename:
  Applied 1 edit(s) to a.ts
  Applied 2 edit(s) to b.ts
```

The declaration becomes `export function respond(): number { return 42; }`, but the exact consumer content is:

```ts
respond
respond
import { answer } from "./a.js";
export const value = answer();
export const newValue = answer();
```

## Platform

Windows (WSL), Linux x64.

## omp version

Source checkout `can1357/oh-my-pi@2ee514247f`, using its production `LspTool.execute` and native module built from that revision. Exact `omp --version` output was not captured.

## Bun version

1.4.0

## Provider

Not provider-specific; direct tool execution.

## Area

Tool execution — LSP.

## Additional context

Source locations at `2ee514247f`:

- [`tool.ts:1165–1168`](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/2ee514247f/packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/tool.ts#L1165-L1168) reconciles only `targetFile`; [`1494–1519`](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/2ee514247f/packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/tool.ts#L1494-L1519) requests rename edits and applies them.
- [`edits.ts:163–166`](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/2ee514247f/packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/edits.ts#L163-L166) reads current disk text; [`29–49`](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/blob/2ee514247f/packages/coding-agent/src/lsp/edits.ts#L29-L49) applies server ranges, including to empty lines, without checking snapshot content.

Related: [#11416](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/issues/11416) / [#11419](https://github.com/can1357/oh-my-pi/pull/11419) fixed reconciliation of the target file itself; here a different, already-open consumer changed.

