## Summary

A hashline `PUT N-M` / `PUT N.=M` whose endpoint splits a syntax node is written to disk. The file parsed before the edit and no longer does. The tool warns and does not roll back, so the next edit runs against shifted line numbers and the damage cascades.

`edit.autoRepair.enabled` defaults to false, and auto-repair is the wrong primary fix anyway (it guesses after the bad write). `edit.enforceSeenLines` does not catch this: the endpoint line was displayed, it just is not a node boundary.

Closed #14170 asked for `edit.rejectOnParseRegression` and was retracted by its author with no PR. The setting does not exist in 18.8.6.

## Environment

- OS: Windows 11, `win32 10.0.26200`, x64. Not WSL. The second warning printed a Windows path (`tmp\wsprobe-b\range-end.tsx`).
- omp: `@oh-my-pi/pi-coding-agent` 18.8.6, edit mode `hashline`. Grammar dumped on the same machine with `bun` via `editGrammar("hashline")` from `@oh-my-pi/pi-natives`.
- `lsp.formatOnWrite: false`. This is not the indent-halving format-on-write bug.
- The `edit:` block in the user config at repro time set `mode: hashline` and did not override `edit.autoRepair.enabled` or `edit.enforceSeenLines`, so the package defaults apply (auto-repair off, seen-line guard on).
- Fixture newline: CRLF. The failure is a mid-node splice, not a CRLF-to-LF rewrite.

## Repro

File (`range-end.tsx`, CRLF, parses):

```tsx
export function Demo() {
  const columns = useMemo(() => {
    const base = [];
    if (canDrill) {
      base.push({ id: "actions" });
    }
    return base;
  }, [
    actions,
    branch,
  ]);
  return base;
}
```

After `read` (snapshot tag minted, lines 7-8 visible), apply:

```text
PUT 7-8:
+    return base;
+  }, [
+    branch,
+  ]);
```

`PUT 7-8` is accepted as a range alias of `PUT 7.=8`. `editInspect("hashline", ...)` accepts both spellings. Result on disk:

```tsx
    return base;
  }, [
    branch,
  ]);
    actions,
    branch,
  ]);
```

The range ended on `}, [` (line 8). The body closed the array. The old tail (`actions`, `branch`, `]);`) was outside the range and survived. Two warnings came back, and the file stayed broken:

> This edit introduced a syntax error near line 7: the file parsed before the patch and no longer does. It was applied exactly as written, so a line number or range endpoint is likely wrong

> Warning: tmp\wsprobe-b\range-end.tsx no longer parses after this edit. The change was applied; re-read the edited region and fix the syntax, or revert if unintended.

Same file, restored, `PUT 2*:` resolved lines 2-11 and stayed valid. The engine already knows the syntax span and does not use it to reject a splitting range.

## Expected

1. If the file parsed before and does not after, do not leave the broken write. Restore `oldText` and return `isError`. `parseRegressed` is already on `EditFileOutcome`.
2. Before apply, if a `PUT` range start or end is not a node boundary, reject and return the resolved span (`range splits CallExpression spanning 2-11; use PUT 2* or PUT 2.=11`). `PUT N*` already computes this span.

Do not treat `edit.autoRepair.enabled` as the fix. It runs after the bad write and can revert or invent.

## Evidence

- One Windows session: 22 warnings, all `PUT N-M` (hyphen, not `.=`), all the same sentence, while editing CRLF `.tsx` / `.test.tsx`. Model: `muse-spark-1.3-contributor` via `opencode-go`. After the first bad write the tool tells the model to re-issue a correcting edit against the new snapshot, which is how the count reached 22.
- Typical bad endpoint in that session: a range ending at `}, [` or at the first line of a `useState(` call, so the closer stayed outside the range.
- Read views elide block bodies with `…`. `edit.enforceSeenLines` only rejects lines never displayed. A visible opener that is not a node boundary still passes.
- Grammar export says `range: LID ".=" LID`. The native parser also accepts `N-M` and applies it as that range.
- Workaround that does not fix the tool: `PUT N*:` for a whole syntax node, or `edit.modelVariants` routing a model that emits splitting ranges to `replace`. Neither rolls back a bad hashline write.
